  "query_preview": "..\nThursday night through Friday: Broad troughing in the northeast \nPacific will keep the Pacific Northwest in a progressive synoptic \npattern through the weekend. First, a shortwave is expected to push \nthrough this evening bringing light showers across far eastern \nWashington and north Idaho. Clearing skies behind this and \ndecreasing winds across northeast Washington will bring a risk for \nfrost, primarily in the sheltered valleys early Friday morning. The \nhighest confidence for frost will be around Winthrop, Twisp, \nRepublic, Colville, Cusick, Usk which is where the NBM has a greater \nthan 80% chance for low temperatures below 36F. Because of this, a \nFrost Advisory has been issued for the Methow Valley, Okanogan \nHighlands, and northeast Washington. Despite being included in the \nFrost Advisory, lower confidence exists for spots along Ferry and \nStevens county border along the Columbia River due to the influence \nfrom the river and around southern Stevens and Pend Oreille counties \ndue to lingering wind overnight. NBM generally has a 40% chance or \nless for low temperatures below 36F in these locations. \n\nAnother shortwave arrives Friday afternoon and evening and brings \nadditional showers across northeast Washington and north Idaho. \nModeled soundings suggest the mid-levels may be a little too stable \nto allow for enough charge separation to support lightning, with a \n10-15% chance for isolated thunderstorms in the afternoon. \n\nFriday night through Sunday: Center of the upper-level low in the \nnortheast Pacific slides into the Pacific Northwest and becomes \ncentered over the Inland Northwest Saturday afternoon. Frost will \nremain a risk across the northern valleys with a 60% chance or \ngreater for temperatures below 36F early Saturday morning. Despite \nthe NBM giving the Columbia Basin, Palouse, and Spokane area a 30% \nchance for temperatures below 36F, breezy winds overnight will be a \nlimiting factor. \n\nBy Saturday afternoon, cooling mid level temperatures associated \nwith the cold pool aloft (500mb temperatures -31C to -28C) will \nsteepen lapse rates, yielding 100-300 J/kg of surface-based CAPE. \nThis environment would support more organized showers and a 15-25% \nchance of isolated thunderstorms across northeast Washington and the \nIdaho Panhandle.\n\nSaturday night through Sunday morning brings the best chances for \nfrost across northeast Washington, north Idaho, the Spokane area, \nand the Palouse with lighter winds anticipated. While lingering \ncloud cover remains a potential mitigation for radiative cooling, the \nNBM gives northeast Washington nearly a 100% chance for low \ntemperatures below 36F Sunday morning, a 70-90% chance across the \nPalouse, and a 50-70% chance for the Spokane/CdA area and up toward \nSandpoint. The potential for freezing conditions is also there \nacross northeast Washington with a 70% chance of temperatures below \n32F around Republic,Colville, Usk, and Deer Park. \n\nMonday through Thursday: Uncertainty in the synoptic pattern begins \nto grow as models diverge on the progression of low as it digs into \nthe central US and an upper-level ridge attempts to build into the \nPacific Northwest. While there is general agreement for a gradual \nwarming trend with 85% of ensembles favoring this, the 25th-75th \npercentile max temperature spread widens significantly from Monday \nto Thursday. This reflects the differences in the timing and \nstrength of this ridge, plus the potential of another system to move \nthrough which is showed in 15% of the ensembles. /vmt",
